    audioTranscriptEn:
      'Step softly beneath the carved sandstone arch of Mukteswar, hailed across the world as the Gem of Odishan Architecture. Around nine hundred and fifty Common Era, under the Somavamshi king Yayati the First, our guild of Ekamra stone masters stood at a creative crossroads. Before us lay the modest rectangular halls of earlier centuries; ahead beckoned the soaring cathedrals of Lingaraj and Konark. Here at Mukteswar, within just thirty-five feet of warm red sandstone, we perfected every jewel of the Kalinga canon. Look upward at the ceremonial Makara Torana—two sixteen-sided columns lifting a semicircular arch from the jaws of mythical sea-monsters, draped with celestial nymphs and beaded garlands.\n\nPeer through the diamond-lattice windows of the Jagamohana, where playful monkeys tease crocodiles in fables from the Panchatantra, and gaze at the ceiling inside, where an eight-petalled lotus holds the Seven Divine Mothers, each cradling an infant. Now walk eastward across the meadow to Rajarani Temple, sculpted seventy years later from luminous amber-gold Rajarani stone. Notice how miniature spires cluster like musical notes rising up its fifty-nine-foot tower. Though its sanctum stands empty today, look at the guardians of the eight directions—Varuna poised upon his makara, Agni wreathed in living stone fire—and the graceful Salabhanjika maidens fastening their anklets as if pausing mid-dance. In Mukteswar and Rajarani, stone ceases to be silent rock; it breathes, smiles, and sings.',
