    qrCodeBadgeText: 'ASI Protected Monument / Rare 9th-Century Hypaethral 64-Yogini Shrine',
    audioTranscriptEn:
      'Bow your head as you pass through the low sandstone threshold of Hirapur, and step out into a circle of pure sky. Around you rises the ninth-century Chausathi Yogini Temple, consecrated by Queen Hiradevi of the Bhauma-Kara dynasty beside the quiet waters of the Bhargavi river. Look upward—there is no spire, no dome, no ceiling of stone. This sanctuary is hypaethral, deliberately open to the heavens, so that sunbeams at dawn, monsoon rain, ocean wind, and midnight starlight fall directly into the sacred mandala.\n\nTurn slowly around the circular wall. From sixty shadowed niches—and four more upon the central Chandi pavilion—sixty-four dark chlorite Yoginis gaze back at you with serene, commanding power. Not one sculpture repeats another. Here stands an archer Yogini drawing her bow atop a charging boar; beside her, a celestial musician strums a vina above a lotus, another balances effortlessly on a scorpion, awheeled crab, an elephant, or a leaping antelope. Look at their intricate coiled chignons, jeweled girdles, and ecstatic dance postures, culminating directly opposite the entrance in ten-armed Goddess Mahamaya. Hidden in quiet village groves until historian Kedarnath Mahapatra walked through this doorway in nineteen fifty-three, Hirapur remains Odisha’s most intimate encounter between polished black stone and the infinite cosmos.',
