    documentedHistory:
      'Known in antiquity as Viraja Kshetra, Guhadevapataka, Guheswarapataka, and Yayatinagara, Jajpur on the southern bank of the sacred Baitarani River served for over six centuries as the political and spiritual capital of ancient Toshali and Utkala. Its earliest literary attestation appears in the Tirthayatra section of the Vana Parva of the Mahabharata (c. 3rd century CE), which extols bathing in the Vaitarani at Viraja Tirtha as purifying an entire lineage. Inside the sanctum of the Biraja Temple (rebuilt in the 13th century over Gupta foundations) stands a unique iconographic treasure of Indian art: a two-armed (Dvibhuja) Mahishasuramardini Durga piercing the chest of a water-buffalo with a trident in her right hand while pulling its tail with her left—dating stylistically to the 4th–5th century CE.\n\nBetween 736 CE and 945 CE, Jajpur served as Guhadevapataka, the imperial capital of the Bhauma-Kara dynasty. Uniquely in South Asian history, six sovereign queens ascended the throne of Jajpur in their own right, assuming full imperial titles of Paramabhattarika Maharajadhiraja Parameswari: Tribhuvana Mahadevi I (845 CE), Tribhuvana Mahadevi II (Prithvi Mahadevi, 894 CE), Gauri Mahadevi, Dandi Mahadevi, Vakula Mahadevi, and Dharma Mahadevi.\n\nIn the 10th century, Somavamshi Emperor Yayati I celebrated the unification of Kosala and Utkala by performing ten horse sacrifices at Dashashwamedha Ghat on the Baitarani, inviting ten thousand Vedic scholars from Kanyakubja (Kannauj) and consecrating the monumental Saptamatrika gallery alongside the 36-foot monolithic Subhastambha (Garuda/Vulture pillar) erected in 966 CE.',
    legendAndFolklore:
      'According to the Brahmanda Purana and Viraja Kshetra Mahatmya, when Lord Brahma performed a cosmic Mahayajna on the banks of the Baitarani, Goddess Parvati emerged directly from the sacrificial altar fire (Garhapatya Agnikunda) as "Viraja" (the Pure and Passionless One). She requested Brahma to establish Jajpur as a supreme Shakti Kshetra crowned with one Koti minus one (99,99,999) Shiva lingas guarded by eight Bhairavas and eight Chandis.\n\nEqually revered is the legend of Gayasura from the Vayu Purana: when Lord Vishnu pressed down the titanic body of the pious demon Gayasura to stabilize the three worlds, Gayasura’s head rested at Gaya in Magadha (Shiro-Gaya), his feet rested at Pithapuram in Andhra (Pada-Gaya), and his navel (Nabhi) rested inside the Biraja Temple complex at Jajpur—creating the bottomless sacred well of Nabhi Gaya where ancestral Pinda-dana offerings grant immediate moksha.',
