    qrCodeBadgeText: 'Adi Shakti Peetha & Nabhi Gaya Tirtha / Ancient Capital Guhadevapataka',
    audioTranscriptEn:
      'Welcome to Jajpur on the shimmering banks of the holy Baitarani River—known in the Mahabharata as Viraja Kshetra, and in imperial copper plates as Guhadevapataka. Long before Cuttack or Bhubaneswar rose as royal seats, Jajpur stood as the beating political heart of ancient Odisha, and the throne of an extraordinary dynasty unlike almost any other in world history. Between the ninth and tenth centuries, six sovereign queens of the Bhauma-Kara empire—beginning with the stateswoman Empress Tribhuvana Mahadevi the First in eight hundred and forty-five—ruled this realm in their own name, issuing royal charters as Paramabhattarika Maharajadhiraja.\n\nStep inside the Biraja Shakti Peetha to behold one of India’s oldest surviving Shakta icons: a fifth-century, uniquely two-armed Goddess Biraja piercing the buffalo demon, her crown bearing Ganesha, a Shiva linga, and the crescent moon—uniting Shakta, Shaiva, and Ganapatya streams in one sacred image. Just steps away lies Nabhi Gaya, the cosmic navel of Gayasura, where families have offered ancestral Pinda-dana for over fifteen hundred years. Down on the Baitarani riverfront at Dashashwamedha Ghat, where Emperor Yayati Keshari kindled ten royal Vedic fire altars, colossal nine-foot Mothers of the Saptamatrika shrine still watch serenely over the flowing waters.',
