    documentedHistory:
      'Rising along the fertile northern bank of the Rushikulya River near Purushottampur in Ganjam district, the monumental fortified citadel of Jaugada—known in Mauryan imperial records as Samapa—served in the 3rd century BCE as the southern provincial headquarters of newly annexed Kalinga, complementing Tosali (Sisupalgarh/Dhauli) in the north. Excavated by archaeologist Debala Mitra of the Archaeological Survey of India in 1956–57, Jaugada revealed a vast quadrangular mud-rampart fortress measuring nearly 800 meters on each side, rising 25 feet high and surrounded by a 100-foot defensive moat, yielding Black-and-Red Ware, punch-marked silver coins, Puri-Kushana copper coins, and carnelian beads.\n\nOn three vertical faces of a towering granite outcrop named Kapakhia inside the fort, Emperor Ashoka (c. 256 BCE) had his scribes chisel Major Rock Edicts I through X and XIV alongside the two Separate Kalinga Edicts—deliberately omitting Edict XIII (which described the carnage of the Kalinga War) out of sensitivity to the grieving populace of Kalinga, substituting instead a solemn judicial charter commanding his Samapa Mahamatras to govern with impartiality and compassion.\n\nJust ten kilometers downstream, where the Rushikulya bends gracefully around the 708-foot Pramoda/Kumari Hill, stands the revered Shrine of Maa Tara Tarini. Celebrated in Shakta Tantras as one of the four primordial Adi Shakti Peethas of India (representing the Stana-Peetha of Devi Sati), the shrine blends prehistoric tribal stone worship, Buddhist Vajrayana Tara devotion of ancient Sadhabas (mariners sailing from nearby Palur/Dantapura), and Rekha-style Kalinga temple architecture.',
    legendAndFolklore:
      'Local lore gives Samapa its modern name "Jaugada" ("Lac Fort"), recounting a folk belief that King Duryodhana built its impregnable walls mixed with molten lac so no arrow could pierce them until torrential rains revealed its secret. Across the river on Kumari Hill, Puranic tradition narrates that after Daksha’s Yajna, when Lord Vishnu’s Sudarshana Chakra severed the sacred body of Devi Sati to calm Shiva’s cosmic Tandava, Sati’s twin breasts fell upon Kumari Parvata on the banks of the Rushikulya (elder sister of the Ganga), manifesting as the twin stone faces of Tara and Tarini.\n\nAnother beloved Ganjam chronicle tells of Basu Praharaj, a devout Brahmin scholar of Kharida village, to whom two radiant little girls appeared as foster daughters before vanishing one night and speaking in a dream: "Do not grieve, father; climb Kumari Hill where we dwell eternally above the Rushikulya to bless sailors, mothers, and children." To this day, hundreds of thousands gather every Tuesday of the Odia month of Chaitra for the grand Chaitra Mela and ceremonial first-haircut (Mundana) rites.',
