    audioTranscriptEn:
      'Stand beside the gleaming granite face of Jaugada in the green Rushikulya valley, and trace with your eyes letters chiselled more than two thousand two hundred years ago. In the third century before the Common Era, this vast earthen-walled fortress was Samapa, southern capital of Mauryan Kalinga. Knowing the deep wounds left by the Kalinga War, Emperor Ashoka deliberately left out his battle chronicle here, inscribing instead a tender constitutional pledge in Brahmi Prakrit: "All men are my children. Just as I desire for my own children that they enjoy every welfare and happiness in this world and the next, so do I desire for all people." He commanded the magistrates of Samapa never to imprison or torment a single citizen without cause.\n\nJust ten kilometers down this same sacred Rushikulya river rises the seven-hundred-foot Kumari Hill, crowned by Maa Tara Tarini—revered across India as one of the four primordial Adi Shakti Peethas. Climb the nine hundred and ninety-nine stone steps, or glide above the canopy, to greet the twin primordial faces of Tara and Tarini, whose blessings once guided ancient Kalinga mariners setting sail from the nearby port of Palur toward Java, Bali, and Sumatra. Here in Ganjam, imperial non-violence and eternal maternal grace flow side by side along the Rushikulya.',
