    documentedHistory:
      'Spanning 672 square kilometers across the estuarine delta of the Brahmani, Baitarani, Pathsala, and Dhamra rivers in Kendrapara district, Bhitarkanika is a rare living tapestry where medieval maritime history and globally vital wetland ecology intertwine. From the late 13th century, this amphibious frontier belonged to the Princely Estate of Kanika, founded around 1290 CE by Raja Bhujabala Bhanja of the Mayurbhanj royal house. Navigating tidal creeks lined with Sundari, Hental (Phoenix paludosa), and Rai mangroves, Kanika’s admirals guarded coastal Kalinga against Arakanese and Portuguese pirates from fortified mud-and-laterite bastions, building the 75-pillared Rajkanika Palace and brick watchtowers at Dangamal and Habalikhati—alongside surviving 11th–12th century stone images of Mahishasuramardini and Shiva Lingas hidden inside Bhitarkanika island.\n\nIn 1921–22, Kanika also wrote a stirring chapter of India’s Non-Cooperation Movement when peasant leader Chakradhar Behera and Gopabandhu Das championed the Kanika Praja Andolan against arbitrary agrarian cesses. Ecologically, in 1975, FAO consultant Dr. H.R. Bustard and Odisha wildlife pioneers launched India’s landmark Saltwater Crocodile Conservation Project at Dangamal, bringing Crocodylus porosus back from near extinction (under 95 individuals in 1975 to over 1,811 today, including rare leucistic white "Sankhua" crocodiles) while protecting Gahirmatha—earth’s largest mass-nesting (Arribada) rookery of Olive Ridley sea turtles.\n\nInland at Kendrapara town ("Tulasi Kshetra"), the magnificent 75-foot Sri Baladevjew Temple—constructed in 1761 CE by Raja Gopaljeew of Kujang and Sadhuenswar Mahapatra with Maratha Subedar Janoji Bhonsle’s sanad—enshrines Lord Balabhadra as supreme sovereign.',
    legendAndFolklore:
      'According to the Brahma Tantra and Tulasi Kshetra Mahatmya, Lord Balarama slew the tyrant demon Kandarasura on the banks of the Gobari river and married the pious maiden Tulasi, blessing Kendrapara as "Gupta Kshetra" and "Tulasi Kshetra." During evening Sandhya Dhupa at Sri Baladevjew, temple suaras prepare golden-fried patties of fresh cottage cheese soaked in thickened, cardamom-scented sweetened milk—Kendrapara Rasabali, honoured with India’s Geographical Indication (GI) tag in 2023.\n\nDeep in the tidal creeks of Bhitarkanika, boatmen and honey-gatherers still bow before the forest shrine of Maa Vana-Durga and Dangamal’s sacred white crocodile ("Gori," hatched in 1975), revered locally as the auspicious conch-hued ("Sankhua") mount of River Goddess Ganga.',
