    documentedHistory:
      'Straddling the border of Bargarh and Bolangir districts in Western Odisha, the flat-topped bauxite-and-khondalite massif of Gandhamardan (rising 3,296 feet) is a monumental crossroads of Buddhist philosophy, proto-Odia epigraphy, Hari-Hara syncretism, and ethnobotanical science. In 639 CE, Chinese scholar-monk Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang) recorded in his Si-Yu-Ki a celebrated five-storeyed monastic university named Po-lo-mo-lo-ki-li (Sanskrit: Parimalagiri, "Fragrant Mountain"), constructed by Satavahana ruler Gautamiputra Yajnasri for the great 2nd-century Madhyamaka philosopher and alchemist-physician Acharya Nagarjuna. Archaeological surveys on the plateau at Bhojpurgarh and Nrusinghanath—highlighted by monolithic four-pillared Buddhist hall columns inside the Nrusinghanath Jagamohana—confirm this ancient monastic stratum.\n\nOn the northern Bargarh slope beside the perennial Chaladhar, Bhimadhar, and Kapildhara cascades stands the 45-foot Rekha Deula of Sri Nrusinghanath. Inside the Jagamohana wall survives one of the earliest dated inscriptions written in archaic Odia script and language: dated Friday, Chaitra Purnima of Vikrama Samvat 1470 (17 March 1413 CE), recording that Chauhan King Vaijaladeva I, son of Vatsarajadeva of Patnagarh, rebuilt the temple to gain religious merit and children. Directly across the sixteen-kilometer plateau on the southern Bolangir slope stands its twin shrine, Harishankar, patronized by Queen Durlabha Devi, uniting Shaiva and Vaishnava streams.\n\nRecognizing over 1,055 floral species, 300 medicinal herbs practiced by traditional Vaidyas, and decades of grassroots tribal environmental stewardship, Odisha officially declared Gandhamardan Hill a Biodiversity Heritage Site in March 2023.',
    legendAndFolklore:
      'Ramayana folklore across Kosala narrates that when Lakshmana lay mortally wounded in Lanka and Hanuman flew southward bearing the Himalayan peak of Dronagiri-Gandhamardan glowing with Sanjeevani herbs, a fragrant fragment fell here between Bargarh and Bolangir, endowing every stream and root of Gandhamardan with healing potency.\n\nUnique in all Hindu iconography is the legend of "Marjara-Keshari" ("Cat-Lion") enshrined in the Nrusinghanath Mahatmya. When the sage Udanka’s daughter Malati was wronged by Ravana and abandoned in the forest, her tears moved Mouse-Mother Mushika, from whom sprang the terrifying demon Mushika Daitya ("Mouse Demon"). Fleeing Lord Rama and Narasimha, Mushika Daitya burrowed deep into a narrow cleft inside Gandhamardan rock where no giant lion could enter. Lord Vishnu therefore assumed the lithe, watchful form of Marjara-Nrusingha—half celestial cat, half lion—sitting eternally alert at the rock tunnel entrance on Nrusimha Chaturdashi (Baisakha Mela) so the demon can never emerge.',
