    documentedHistory:
      'Across a dramatic undulating sheet of bare grey granite between Ranipur and Jharial villages in Bolangir district spreads "Soma Tirtha"—one of medieval India’s rarest multi-denominational sacred landscapes, where Shaivism, Tantric Shaktism, Vaishnavism, and Vajrayana Buddhism flourished side by side between the 8th and 11th centuries CE under the Panduvamshi and Somavamshi kings of Dakshina Kosala. First surveyed for modern scholarship by J.D. Beglar (assistant to Sir Alexander Cunningham) in 1874–75, the granite outcrop originally held at least one hundred and twenty stone shrines ringing the sacred Soma Sagar tank; more than fifty Rekha and Khakhara shrines still dot the rock face today.\n\nCrowning the crest is India’s second intact circular roofless Chausathi Yogini Temple (50 feet outer diameter, larger than Hirapur), built around 900 CE. At the exact center of the open circle stands a four-pillared stone pavilion enshrining a magnificent three-headed, eight-armed dancing Shiva (Nataraja) accompanied by Ganesha and Nandi, surrounded by sixty-four sunken niches holding ecstatic dancing Yoginis—remarkably, over a dozen bearing zoomorphic heads of lioness, mare, sow, serpent, cat, and elephant.\n\nJust north of the rock sheet rises the breathtaking 75-foot (23-meter) Indralath Temple, constructed in the 10th century from finely dressed, kiln-fired terracotta bricks carved after laying with Vaishnava icons of Narasimha, Varaha, and Krishna-Kaliya. Nearby, the Someshwara Shiva Temple preserves a crystal-clear Sanskrit lintel inscription of the celebrated Mattamayura Shaiva Siddha master Acharya Gaganasiva.',
    legendAndFolklore:
      'Oral tradition explains the twin name Ranipur-Jharial through royal devotion: a Somavamshi queen ("Rani") founded the sovereign settlement of Ranipur while constructing a ring of sacred stepped reservoirs ("Jharial") fed by subterranean springs beneath the granite dome so that pilgrims bathing in Soma Tirtha would be cured of skin and lunar afflictions—just as Soma (the Moon God) was healed here according to the Vamana Purana.\n\nVillagers call the towering brick spire "Indralath" ("Staff of Lord Indra"), recounting that Indra himself descended at midnight with celestial Vishwakarma bricklayers to raise a crimson tower of baked earth before cockcrow, embedding fine paddy husks and herbal lime mortar so resilient that nine hundred monsoons have failed to topple its 75-foot crown.',
