    documentedHistory:
      'Poised dramatically on a triangular peninsula where the mighty Mahanadi meets the crystal waters of the Tel River, Suvarnapura (modern Sonepur, headquarters of Subarnapur district) is celebrated as the "Second Varanasi of Odisha" for its constellation of more than a hundred temples—Suvarnameru, Rameswar,ureshwar/Sureswari, Khambeswari, Gopalji, and Samaleswari. Between the 9th and 12th centuries CE, Suvarnapura served successively as the strategic royal seat of the Somavamshis of Kosala, the Bhanjas of Khinjali-mandala, the Kalachuris, and the Telugu-Chodas.\n\nA crucial copper-plate charter of Somavamshi prince Kumara Someswaradeva issued from Suvarnapura explicitly designates this riverine citadel as "Paschima-Lanka" ("Western Lanka"), presided over by Goddess Lankeswari—whose sacred rock crops up directly from a deep mid-channel whirlpool ("Lankeswari Daraha") of the Mahanadi, reachable only by boat and inscribed with 9th–10th century shell-script (Sankhalipi) and Nagari characters. Tibetan Buddhist histories of Taranatha link Paschima-Lanka (Sambhala/Lankapuri) with 8th–9th century Siddha king Indrabhuti and his brilliant sister Princess Lakshmikara, pioneer of Sahajayana Buddhist Tantra and Charyapada mystic poetry.\n\nTwelve kilometers up the Tel River at Baidyanath stands the 9th-century Kosaleswar Shiva Temple, famed across Indian art history for its brick-and-stone pillared mandapa carved with radiant Kartikeya on a peacock, Ardhanariswara, and Ganga-Yamuna; while across at Khaliapali village stands the 1895 Samadhi Peetha of Saint-Poet Bhima Bhoi.',
    legendAndFolklore:
      'According to the Suvarnameru Mahatmya, when impoverished devotees prayed to Lord Shiva while Parvati pitied their hardship, a rain of pure gold coins ("Suvarna-vrishti") showered for two and a half hours over the Mahanadi-Tel confluence—giving the town its name Suvarnapura and raising the golden mound of Suvarnameru Mahadeva.\n\nEqually captivating is the syncretic tradition of Maa Stambheswari (Khambeswari) and the Bali Jatra (Dussehra midnight spirit-possession ritual) of Sonepur: honoring the prehistoric tribal post-goddess adopted since the 5th century CE by Tustikara, Sulki, Bhanja, and Chauhan kings, and woven into every geometric fish, conch, rudraksha, and lotus motif of GI-recognized Sonepuri Bomkai and Pasapali handloom sarees.',
