    documentedHistory:
      'High on the emerald tableland of the Eastern Ghats at 2,900 feet elevation, Koraput preserves both the medieval royal heritage of ancient Jeypore-Nandapur and the deepest indigenous roots of Odisha’s Jagannath consciousness. Forty-five kilometers south of Koraput town lies Nandapur—for over four centuries (13th to mid-17th century CE) the fortified mountain capital of the Silavamsi and Suryavamsi rulers of Kalinga-Jeypore until Maharaja Viravikrama Deva shifted the capital to Jeypore around 1649–1650 CE. At Nandapur still stands the celebrated "Batisa Simhasana" (32-stepped throne well and stone steps featuring thirty-two carved celestial effigies inspired by King Vikramaditya’s throne), alongside a six-foot monolithic Ganesha at Sarveswara Temple and Kalinga-Nagara stone inscriptions.\n\nFurther southwest along the Odisha-Andhra canyon, the Machkund River plunges 175 meters (574 feet) sheer down volcanic cliffs at Duduma Falls (ancient Matsya Tirtha), flanked on its misty eastern escarpment by the Mudulipada highlands of the Bonda (Remo) Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group, whose Austroasiatic Munda language and handwoven ringa skirts reach back millennia.\n\nIn Koraput town itself rises Sabara Srikhetra—initiated in 1972 from an indigenous Sabara hilltop pedestal and expanded into a soaring Rekha temple complex—where tribal Sabara priests (Nayaks/Pujaris) serve alongside Vedic Brahmins, tribal Dhemsa dancers lead Lord Jagannath’s Ratha Yatra, and human beings of every caste, faith, and nation are welcomed inside the sanctum courtyard.',
    legendAndFolklore:
      'Skanda Purana and Sarala Das’s 15th-century Odia Mahabharata both proclaim that before Lord Jagannath ever dwelt in Puri’s stone Srimandira, He was worshipped in secret forest caves as sapphire Nilamadhava by the tribal Sabara chieftain Viswavasu and his daughter Lalita, offered wild forest berries, honey, and roots. Sabara Srikhetra restores that original covenant of love without social barriers.\n\nAt Duduma Falls ("Matsya Tirtha"), Puranic and Bonda-Gadaba legends meet: it is said that Lord Vishnu in Matsya (Fish) Avatar blessed the sacred Mahseer fish of the Machkund gorge, and that when Chaitanya Mahaprabhu passed through southern Odisha, pilgrims marvelled at rainbow mists rising five hundred feet from the canyon floor. At Nandapur, storytellers recount that no unjust ruler could ever climb all thirty-two carved steps of the Batisa Simhasana without the stone puttalikas questioning his dharma.',
